  <!-- Help topics (Bureau.Common HelpWindow), in the order HelpTopicKeys lists them. Bodies are
       plain text: a blank line starts a new paragraph, and a "keys | action" line is a row of the
       shortcut table, under the line directly above the rows. -->
  <data name="HelpGettingStartedTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Getting started</value></data>
  <data name="HelpGettingStartedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Select one or more PDFs in File Explorer, or a folder, right-click and choose Export pages as images. A folder adds the PDFs directly inside it, not the ones in its subfolders.

Every page shows as a thumbnail, and every page starts ticked. Untick the pages you don't want, pick a format and resolution under Options, and press Save.

To add more PDFs, drag them onto the window or use the round + button. With nothing open, use Choose PDFs.

The PDFs are listed on the left. Click one to jump to its pages, drag it to change the order, or remove it with its ✕.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpChoosingPagesTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choosing pages</value></data>
  <data name="HelpChoosingPagesBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Only ticked pages are saved. Click a page's tick box to include it or leave it out.

The Tick menu ticks All pages or No pages, swaps ticked and unticked, or ticks only the Odd pages or Even pages, which helps with double-sided scans.

To tick particular pages, type them in the Pages box, such as 1-4, 7, 12-, and press Tick range or Enter. 12- means page 12 to the end. The pages you type are ticked and the rest are unticked.

With several PDFs open, these choices apply to every PDF, and page numbers count within each one.

Undo, or Ctrl+Z, takes back the last change: ticks, rotations, and removing or reordering PDFs.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpRotatePreviewTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rotate and preview</value></data>
  <data name="HelpRotatePreviewBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rotate a page 90° with the arrows on its tile. Or click a page to select it (Ctrl+click or Shift+click to select more) and press R to turn them right or Shift+R to turn them left.

Rotating changes only the saved image. The PDF itself is never changed.

Double-click a page, or use its magnifier, to see it full size. In the preview, the arrow keys step through the pages, R and Shift+R rotate, Include this page ticks or unticks it, and Esc closes it.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpResolutionTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Resolution</value></data>
  <data name="HelpResolutionBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Resolution, in Options, sets how many pixels each inch of the page gets (DPI, dots per inch). More dots mean a sharper image and a larger file.

Screen (96 DPI): for viewing on screen. An A4 page is about 790 × 1120 pixels.
Good (150 DPI): the default, sharp enough for most uses. About 1240 × 1750 pixels.
Print (300 DPI): for printing. About 2480 × 3510 pixels.
Maximum (600 DPI): for fine detail. About 4960 × 7020 pixels, and slow to save.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpFormatsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Formats</value></data>
  <data name="HelpFormatsBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>PNG keeps text and lines sharp, with no loss. It is the best choice for most documents.

JPEG makes smaller files, and suits scans and photos. JPEG quality sets the trade: lower is smaller, higher looks better.

TIFF has no loss, and suits archives and printing.

Transparent background works with PNG only: the white of the page is left see-through. JPEG and TIFF always have a white background.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpWhereImagesGoTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Where the images go</value></data>
  <data name="HelpWhereImagesGoBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Save to, in Options, has three choices:

A folder named after each PDF: a new folder beside the PDF, so the pages of report.pdf go into a folder called report. This is the default.
The same folder as each PDF: the images sit beside the PDF.
A folder I choose: every image goes into one folder. Pick it with Choose folder.

If images with the same names are already there, you're asked before they are replaced.

When the save is done, the message bar offers Open and Show in folder.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpFileNamesTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>File names</value></data>
  <data name="HelpFileNamesBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>File names, in Options, is a pattern. These words in braces are filled in for each image:

{name}: the PDF's file name
{page}: the page number
{total}: how many pages the PDF has
{dpi}: the resolution

The default, {name}-p{page}, gives report-p007.png for page 7 of a 200-page PDF. Page numbers get leading zeros, so the files sort in page order everywhere. The example under the box shows what the first page will be called.

If two pages would get the same name, the app says so and how to fix it, such as adding {page} or {name}. Two PDFs with the same name saved to one folder are told apart as report and report (2).</value></data>

  <data name="HelpCopyPageTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy a page</value></data>
  <data name="HelpCopyPageBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy this page, on a page's tile, puts that page on the clipboard as an image, ready to paste into an email or a document. Nothing is saved to a file.

It uses the resolution from Options and the page's rotation, so it matches what Save would give.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpCancellingTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cancelling a save</value></data>
  <data name="HelpCancellingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>While a save runs, a progress bar shows how many pages are done. Cancel stops it.

Until the save ends, the list of PDFs, the pages' ticks and rotations, and Options can't be changed, and Undo waits too. You can still scroll, preview pages and copy a page.

Nothing is left behind: the images are only put in place once every page has been made, so a cancelled save leaves no images, and no existing ones are replaced.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpPasswordsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Password-protected PDFs</value></data>
  <data name="HelpPasswordsBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>A PDF that needs a password asks for it once, naming the PDF. The password is used only to open it, and is never saved anywhere.

Skip, or a wrong password, leaves that PDF out, and the other PDFs open as usual. The message bar lists it as skipped.

Windows can't open PDFs that use AES-256 encryption, so they are listed as skipped without asking.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpSkippedFilesTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Skipped and unreadable files</value></data>
  <data name="HelpSkippedFilesBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The message bar at the bottom lists files that weren't added, and why: a file that isn't a PDF, a PDF that's already open, or a protected PDF that couldn't be opened.

A PDF that is damaged or can't be read shows Couldn't open this PDF under its heading, with Windows' reason when it gives one. The other PDFs load as usual.

A single page that can't be shown says so on its tile. It is left out of the save, and the message bar names it.</value></data>

  <data name="HelpPrivacyTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Privacy</value></data>
  <data name="HelpPrivacyBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Everything happens on this PC. The app never connects to the internet, and sends nothing anywhere.

Your PDFs are only read, never changed, moved or deleted. Passwords are never saved. Your Options are remembered on this PC, and a log for fixing problems stays on this PC too (About, Diagnostics).</value></data>

  <data name="HelpKeyboardShortcutsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keyboard shortcuts</value></data>
  <data name="HelpKeyboardShortcutsBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>In the main window
F1 | Open Help
Enter | Save the ticked pages
Ctrl+Z | Undo
R | Rotate the selected pages right
Shift+R | Rotate the selected pages left

In the preview
Left / Right | Previous or next page
R | Rotate right
Shift+R | Rotate left
Esc | Close the preview

In the Pages box
Enter | Tick the range you typed</value></data>
</root>
